---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ToolRoster from '../../components/ToolRoster.astro';
import RelatedTools from '@kit/components/RelatedTools.astro';
import { SITE, toolBySlug, toolsExcept } from '../../lib/site';

const tool = toolBySlug('gpa-calculator')!;
const canonical = `/tools/${tool.slug}`;
const title = `${tool.title} | ${SITE.name}`;
const description =
  'Work out your GPA with any number of courses. Shows weighted and ' +
  'unweighted side by side, handles honours, AP, IB and dual enrolment, and ' +
  'keeps the working so you can check it.';

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: tool.title, url: `${SITE.url}${canonical}`,
    applicationCategory: 'EducationalApplication', operatingSystem: 'Any',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: [
      {
        '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is the difference between weighted and unweighted GPA?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text:
          'Unweighted GPA puts every course on the same 4.0 scale regardless of difficulty. ' +
          'Weighted GPA adds extra grade points for harder courses — commonly 0.5 for honours ' +
          'and 1.0 for AP or IB — so it can exceed 4.0. Most college applications recalculate ' +
          'an unweighted GPA using their own rules, while your school reports the weighted one ' +
          'on your transcript and usually builds class rank from it.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question', name: 'How do you calculate GPA?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text:
          'Multiply each course grade point by the credits that course carries, add those ' +
          'products together, then divide by the total credits. A one-credit A- and a ' +
          'three-credit B do not count equally, which is why credits matter as much as grades.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question', name: 'Does an A+ count as 4.3?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text:
          'At most US institutions no — the unweighted scale caps at 4.0 and an A+ counts the ' +
          'same as an A. A minority of schools award 4.3. This calculator uses 4.0, because ' +
          'assuming the higher value would inflate a GPA in the direction people want it to go.' },
      },
    ],
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout {title} {description} {canonical} {schema}>
  <ToolRoster
    title="What is my GPA?"
    intro="Add your courses. Both the weighted and the unweighted figure update as you type, because which one you need depends entirely on who is asking."
    breadcrumbs={[{ href: '/', label: 'Home' }, { href: '/tools', label: 'Calculators' }, { label: 'GPA' }]}
    rosterId="gpa"
    addLabel="Add a course"
    columns="minmax(0,1fr) 82px 92px 132px"
  >
    <Fragment slot="result">
      <div class="scorecard__label">Unweighted GPA</div>
      <div class="scorecard__value" id="gpa-un">—</div>
      <p class="scorecard__sub" id="gpa-sub">—</p>
      <div class="scorecard__split">
        <div class="stat">
          <span class="stat__label">Weighted</span>
          <span class="scorecard__alt" id="gpa-w">—</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <span class="stat__label">Credits</span>
          <span class="scorecard__alt" id="gpa-cr">—</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="roster">
      <span>Course</span><span>Credits</span><span>Grade</span><span>Level</span>
    </Fragment>

    <div class="working">
      <div class="working__head">The working</div>
      <div class="working__body" id="gpa-working"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="verdict">
      <div class="verdict__label">In one sentence</div>
      <p class="verdict__text" id="gpa-verdict">—</p>
    </div>

    <div id="gpa-notes"></div>

    <section class="prose" style="margin-top:var(--s-8)">
      <h2>Two numbers, and why you get both</h2>
      <p>
        A GPA is a credit-weighted average of grade points. Multiply each
        course's grade point by its credits, add the results, divide by total
        credits. That last step is the one people skip: a one-credit A and a
        four-credit C do not cancel out, and a calculator that ignores credits
        will flatter you.
      </p>
      <p>
        The harder question is which GPA. <strong>Unweighted</strong> puts
        every course on the same 4.0 scale — an A in gym and an A in AP
        Chemistry both count 4.0. <strong>Weighted</strong> adds points for
        harder courses, so it can and routinely does exceed 4.0.
      </p>
      <p>
        You need both because different readers want different ones. Your
        school reports the weighted figure and usually builds class rank from
        it. Most college applications recalculate an unweighted GPA using their
        own rules — often stripping out non-academic courses entirely. Many
        scholarship formulas and athletic eligibility rules specify unweighted.
        A tool that shows one number has quietly chosen for you.
      </p>

      <h2>The weighting is a local convention, not a standard</h2>
      <p>
        There is no national weighting scale. This calculator applies the most
        common convention — nothing extra for a regular course, +0.5 for
        honours, +1.0 for AP, IB and dual enrolment — because it is what most
        schools do. But plenty do something else: a flat +1.0 for anything
        above regular, a 5.0 scale throughout, weighting confined to certain
        departments, or caps on how many weighted courses count.
      </p>
      <p>
        So treat the weighted figure here as an estimate and your school's
        published scale as the authority. If the two disagree, your school is
        right. The unweighted figure is far more portable, which is part of
        why applications prefer it.
      </p>

      <h2>What this deliberately does not do</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>It does not decide which courses count.</strong> Colleges
        recalculating your GPA frequently drop physical education, health and
        electives, and count only core academic subjects. Whether a course
        belongs in the calculation is a judgement this page cannot make for
        you — it averages what you enter.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>It does not handle pass/fail, withdrawals or repeats.</strong>
        A pass/fail course usually carries credit but no grade points and
        should be left out. A repeated course may replace the original grade or
        average with it, depending on the institution. Both rules are local.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>A+ counts as 4.0, not 4.3.</strong> Most US institutions cap
        the unweighted scale at 4.0. A minority award 4.3 for an A+, and if
        yours is one of them your real figure will be slightly higher than the
        one here. We chose the lower value deliberately: an error that inflates
        a GPA is the more damaging direction to be wrong in, because it is the
        direction nobody checks.
      </p>

      <h2>Why a GPA gets harder to move every term</h2>
      <p>
        This is the arithmetic students most often get wrong, and it is worth
        seeing plainly. A GPA is an average over every credit you have taken,
        so each new term is a smaller share of the total. Twelve credits at a
        3.0 average, followed by a perfect 4.0 term of twelve credits, gives
        3.5 — not 3.7, and not "nearly a 4.0".
      </p>
      <p>
        After sixty credits at 3.0, a flawless thirty-credit year lifts you to
        about 3.33. The maths is unforgiving in one direction and merciful in
        the other: a bad term early does lasting damage, and a bad term late
        barely registers. If you are trying to clear a threshold by a
        particular date, work out how many credits you have left before you
        decide whether the target is reachable — the number of terms remaining
        matters more than the effort in any one of them.
      </p>

      <h2>Semester GPA and cumulative GPA are different questions</h2>
      <p>
        A semester GPA covers one term. A cumulative GPA covers everything.
        Institutions use them for different things — academic probation and
        dean's list usually key off the semester figure, while graduation
        honours, transfer admission and most scholarships use the cumulative.
      </p>
      <p>
        To roll a new term into an existing record you cannot average the two
        GPAs; you have to weight them by credits. Prior GPA times prior
        credits, plus term GPA times term credits, divided by the total. This
        page computes a single set of courses; to get a cumulative figure,
        enter every course you have taken, or add one row standing for your
        prior record with its total credits and its average grade.
      </p>

      <h2>Three mistakes worth avoiding</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Entering percentages instead of letters.</strong> An 89% is not
        3.89. Grade points come from the letter your school assigned, and the
        percentage-to-letter cut-offs are local — an 89 is a B+ in most places
        and an A- in some. Convert using your school's scale, then enter the
        letter.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Guessing at credits.</strong> A year-long high school course is
        usually one credit and a semester course a half; at university a course
        is typically three or four. Guessing here distorts the average more
        than a misremembered grade does, because credits multiply everything.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Counting courses the reader will not.</strong> If you are
        checking against a college's threshold, work out their rules first.
        Many strip electives and count only core academic subjects, which can
        move a GPA by a couple of tenths in either direction.
      </p>

      <h2>What a GPA is worth, and what it is not</h2>
      <p>
        GPA is the single most portable summary of academic record, which is
        why it is asked for so often. It is also crude. It cannot distinguish
        an upward trajectory from a downward one at the same average, it
        rewards course selection as much as performance, and comparing figures
        across schools with different scales and different grading cultures is
        close to meaningless.
      </p>
      <p>
        The practical use is narrow and real: checking you are above a
        threshold. Scholarship minimums, athletic eligibility, honour roll,
        the cut-off for a particular programme. For that, the number needs to
        be right, and it needs to be the <em>right kind</em> of number — which
        is the whole reason this page shows two.
      </p>
    </section>

    <RelatedTools heading="Once the grades are settled" items={toolsExcept(tool.slug).map((t) => ({
      href: `/tools/${t.slug}`, title: t.title, desc: t.blurb,
    }))} />
  </ToolRoster>

  <script src="../../scripts/gpa-island.ts"></script>
</BaseLayout>
